  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM MDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nWednesday is looking to be our final day of unseasonably warm \ntemperatures for the county warning area (CWA). High temperatures \nare forecast in the 90s to 100s Wednesday. Winds will overall be \nlight, but afternoon gusts around 25 mph are possible for the \nnorthwest CWA. After Wednesday, our region is forecast to \ntransition to a weak ridging pattern aloft with ensembles showing a \npersistent surface low setting up somewhere in western Kansas. This \nwill start a much needed cooling trend for the CWA. High \ntemperatures are forecast in the 80s to 90s for Thursday. The \ncooling trend continues through the weekend with high temperatures \nforecast in the 70s to 80s Friday through Monday. \n\nStarting Wednesday afternoon and continuing through the end of the \nforecast period, chances for precipitation are plentiful throughout \nthe entire CWA. Currently, the the Storm Prediction Center (SPC) has \nour CWA outlooked for varying degrees of severe weather potential \nwith the northern CWA being the highest at a slight risk of severe \nweather for Wednesday. Model soundings are showing less than 1000 \nJ/kg of CAPE, 0-6 km shear around 30-40 knots and strong low to mid \nlevel lapse rates. Main hazards are expected to be large hail and \nsevere winds if storms are able to form. Where the best chances for \nsevere weather to occur will ultimately be dependent on where \nin western Kansas the surface low sets up. If the low sets up a\nbit further south, severe weather coverage could increase south\nof I-70. Model soundings are also showing precipitable water \nvalues above 1 inch throughout the CWA. Heavy rainfall and \nflooding potential will be a concern for any storms that form, \nregardless of severity. Slow moving storms and any training \nstorms will have the highest potential for flooding. \n\nThursday through at least Sunday, chances for showers and \nthunderstorms continue for the area due to multiple rounds of \nembedded shortwaves passing through the region. The late afternoons \nand evenings will be our best chances for precipitation each day with \nPoPs maxing out around 80% for the western CWA and decreasing \nfurther east. With multiple days of widespread precipitation in the \nforecast, flooding potential will need to be monitored.",
